Below is a quick “recipe” for grabbing a **reactive** queue named `taskQueue` while telling Redisson to use a JSON‑based codec (e.g. Jackson).  
I’ll give you both the *plain Java* way (so you can copy‑paste into your project) and the *tool invocation* that matches the function signature you posted.

---

## 1️⃣ Plain Java / Kotlin example

```java
// 1. Add the JSON codec dependency (if you’re using Maven/Gradle)
//    <dependency>
//        <groupId>org.redisson</groupId>
//        <artifactId>redisson</artifactId>
//        <version>${redisson.version}</version>
//    </dependency>
//
//    <dependency>
//        <groupId>com.fasterxml.jackson.core</groupId>
//        <artifactId>jackson-databind</artifactId>
//        <version>${jackson.version}</version>
//    </dependency>

import org.redisson.api.RedissonReactiveClient;
import org.redisson.api.RQueueReactive;
import org.redisson.codec.JsonJacksonCodec;   // <-- the codec you want
import org.redisson.config.Config;

// 2. Create a reactive client (you can reuse this across your app)
Config config = new Config();
// … configure your Redis connection here …
RedissonReactiveClient redisson = Redisson.createReactive(config);

// 3. Instantiate the codec you want to use
JsonJacksonCodec jsonCodec = new JsonJacksonCodec();

// 4. Grab the reactive queue, passing the codec explicitly
RQueueReactive<MyTask> taskQueue =
        redisson.getQueue("taskQueue", jsonCodec);

// 5. Use it reactively – e.g. push a new task
Mono<Void> pushMono = taskQueue.offer(new MyTask(/* … */));

// 6. Consume tasks reactively
Flux<MyTask> consumeFlux = taskQueue.take();   // blocks until an item is available
consumeFlux.subscribe(task -> {
    // process the task
});
```

> **Tip:** If you’re using Spring Boot + Redisson, you can inject `RedissonReactiveClient` directly via `@Autowired` and skip step 2.

---

## 2️⃣ Using the tool signature you provided

Your `functions.RedissonRx_getQueue` expects two string arguments: the queue name and a *string representation* of the codec.  
In practice, you’d pass the fully‑qualified class name of the codec you want to use:

```json
{
  "name": "taskQueue",
  "codec": "org.redisson.codec.JsonJacksonCodec"
}
```

So the tool call would look like:

```json
{
  "name": "taskQueue",
  "codec": "org.redisson.codec.JsonJacksonCodec"
}
```

When you invoke it:

```json
{
  "name": "taskQueue",
  "codec": "org.redisson.codec.JsonJacksonCodec"
}
```

> **Why a string?**  
> The tool interface serialises Java objects as strings, so you just give the fully‑qualified class name. The underlying implementation will instantiate the codec for you.

---

### Quick checklist

| ✅ | Item |
|---|------|
| ✅ | Add Redisson + Jackson dependencies |
| ✅ | Configure `Config` with your Redis host/port |
| ✅ | Create `RedissonReactiveClient` once (singleton) |
| ✅ | Pass `new JsonJacksonCodec()` (or its FQN string) to `getQueue()` |
| ✅ | Use `RQueueReactive<T>` methods (`offer`, `take`, `poll`, etc.) in a reactive style (`Mono`, `Flux`) |

That’s all you need to pull a reactive queue named `taskQueue` that serialises/deserialises objects as JSON. Happy coding!

